 3. Brace for impact 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Space weapons are real, and the U.S. has them in orbit right now, Air Force Secretary Troy Meink said at Air, Space &amp; Cyber.

 Why it matters: The declaration is a first — and marks a significant change in the way the world discusses such systems, considered highly sensitive and provocative.

 Friction point: Meink, when pressed by reporters, would not elaborate on what these astral weapons are capable of, how many exist, or if they've been tested.

 "There are a lot of factors that go into how we talk about these things . We spend a lot of time talking about how we're going to talk about these things," Meink said. "Me talking about the details of what we're doing would actually not benefit deterrence." Context: The Trump administration has expressed interest in both the exploration and weaponization of space.

 Militaries rely on the domain for navigation, long-haul communications, surveillance, targeting, missile tracking and more. Flashback: The U.S. in 1962 executed Starfish Prime, a nuclear burst in space. China and Russia blew up their own satellites in 2007 and 2021, respectively. 

 "Is there any question in the room — by anybody — whether or not the Chinese and Russians have been developing these sorts of weapons?" Meink said. "We are focused on making sure we can protect ourselves and operate in that environment, as we've done for decades." More from Axios: 

